---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/asbj/asbj11.json";
---
<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc items={[
        {id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage"},
        {id: "s2", label: "Scope"},
        {id: "s3", label: "Who Counts as a Related Party"},
        {id: "s4", label: "What Must Be Disclosed"},
        {id: "s5", label: "Quantitative Materiality Thresholds – a J-GAAP-Specific Feature"},
        {id: "s6", label: "Worked Example – Identifying a Related Party Transaction"},
        {id: "s7", label: "Worked Example – Applying a Materiality Threshold"},
        {id: "s8", label: "Disclosure Requirements"},
        {id: "s9", label: "Critical Judgments"},
        {id: "s10", label: "Relationship to IAS/IFRS"},
        {id: "s11", label: "Statement Mapping"},
        {id: "s12", label: "Related Standards"},
        {id: "s13", label: "Glossary"}
    ]} />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Transparency for transactions that may not reflect arm's-length terms</div><div class="principle">ASBJ Statement No. 11 requires disclosure of TRANSACTIONS and outstanding BALANCES with RELATED PARTIES – parties who may be able to INFLUENCE, or be INFLUENCED by, the reporting entity in ways that could affect the TERMS of a transaction. Because such relationships CAN result in transactions on terms DIFFERENT from what unrelated parties would negotiate, TRANSPARENT disclosure allows readers to assess the entity's financial position and performance with FULL knowledge of these relationships.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">All companies with related party transactions or relationships</div><div class="principle">This Statement applies to ALL companies with RELATED PARTY relationships or transactions, requiring disclosure REGARDLESS of whether a price was actually charged for the transaction.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Who Counts as a Related Party</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Parent, subsidiaries, affiliates, key individuals, and entities they control</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-1-2"><span>Related party type</span><span>Examples</span></div>
<div class="tr cols-1-2"><span>Group entities</span><span>Parent company, subsidiaries, fellow subsidiaries, affiliates (equity-method investees)</span></div>
<div class="tr alt cols-1-2"><span>Individuals</span><span>Directors, auditors (company auditors), executive officers, and their close family members</span></div>
<div class="tr cols-1-2"><span>Controlled entities</span><span>Companies substantially controlled by directors/officers or their close family members</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>What Must Be Disclosed</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Nature, terms, and amounts of transactions and balances</div><div class="text-supporting">For each MATERIAL related party relationship or transaction, disclosure includes: the NATURE of the relationship; a DESCRIPTION of the transaction(s); the TRANSACTION amount; the ACCOUNTING policy/pricing method applied; OUTSTANDING balances at the reporting date and their terms; and any provision for doubtful accounts related to those balances.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Quantitative Materiality Thresholds – a J-GAAP-Specific Feature</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">More precisely specified thresholds than IFRS's general materiality concept</div><div class="text-supporting">A DISTINCTIVE feature of J-GAAP's implementation guidance is the use of MORE PRECISELY SPECIFIED quantitative thresholds for determining WHICH related party transactions require disclosure – for example, thresholds expressed as a PERCENTAGE of net assets or a SPECIFIC yen amount for particular transaction types (e.g. loans, guarantees) – providing PREPARERS with more CONCRETE, less subjectively-judged guidance than IFRS's more GENERALLY stated, principles-based "materiality" concept under IAS 24.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example – Identifying a Related Party Transaction</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A loan between a company and its affiliate</div>
<div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example: An intercompany loan to an equity-method affiliate</div><div class="wk-body">A company lends ¥300,000,000 to an affiliate accounted for under the equity method, at an interest rate below the affiliate's normal market borrowing rate.</div><div class="wk-answer">This is a RELATED PARTY TRANSACTION requiring disclosure – the AFFILIATE relationship, the ¥300,000,000 loan amount, the BELOW-MARKET interest rate charged, and the outstanding BALANCE at the reporting date must ALL be disclosed, since the terms may not reflect what an UNRELATED lender would have offered.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example – Applying a Materiality Threshold</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Determining whether a transaction crosses the disclosure threshold</div>
<div class="worked worked--flush"><div class="wk-title">Example: A small routine purchase from a director-controlled company</div><div class="wk-body">A company purchases office supplies worth ¥800,000 during the year from a small company controlled by one of its directors. The applicable quantitative threshold for this transaction type is ¥10,000,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the ¥800,000 transaction FALLS BELOW the specified ¥10,000,000 threshold, it does NOT require separate disclosure under this Statement's guidance – illustrating how the MORE PRECISE, quantitative threshold approach gives preparers CLEARER practical guidance than a purely qualitative materiality judgement would.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASBJ Statement No. 11 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature of the related party relationship</span><span>Description, amount, and pricing basis of transactions</span><span>Outstanding balances, terms, and any related doubtful account provisions</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Critical Judgments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Identifying all parties that meet the related party definition, including indirect control/influence relationships</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Generally minimal – this Statement is primarily a disclosure standard rather than a measurement standard</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – J-GAAP vs IAS 24</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-12-12-12"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">J-GAAP (ASBJ Statement No. 11)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IAS 24)</span></div>
<div class="tr cols-12-12-12"><span>Related party definition</span><span>Broadly similar scope (group entities, key individuals, controlled entities)</span><span>Similar scope – converged</span></div>
<div class="tr alt cols-12-12-12"><span>Materiality approach</span><span>More precisely specified quantitative thresholds</span><span>General, principles-based materiality judgement</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">The OVERALL disclosure objective and related party scope are CONVERGED with IAS 24 – the practical difference lies mainly in J-GAAP's preference for MORE CONCRETE, quantitative disclosure thresholds.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every related party disclosure lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Related party transaction/balance</span><span>Notes only</span><span>Disclosed regardless of the underlying transaction's own statement classification</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>ASBJ Statement No. 22</span><span>Consolidated Financial Statements – supplies the subsidiary/affiliate scope used to identify related parties</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Related party</div><div class="glossary-def">A party able to influence, or be influenced by, the reporting entity in ways that could affect transaction terms, including group entities, key individuals, and entities they control.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item glossary-item--last"><div class="glossary-term">Quantitative materiality threshold</div><div class="glossary-def">A specific, precisely stated numerical threshold (e.g. a percentage of net assets or a fixed yen amount) determining which related party transactions require disclosure.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (Japan GAAP terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet (貸借対照表) <span class="badge bp">P&amp;L</span> Statement of Income / Profit and Loss Statement (損益計算書) <span class="badge bo">Net Assets</span> 純資産の部 <span class="badge be">SCNA</span> Statement of Changes in Net Assets (株主資本等変動計算書) <span class="badge bc">SCF</span> Statement of Cash Flows (キャッシュ・フロー計算書)</div></div>

  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
